<interrogationes unitas="actio">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I make a widget do something when it is clicked or receives a key?
    <responsum symbolum="componens_ponere_actio actio_registrare ActioFn" via="lib/dispensator.c">Name
      the action in the tree with componens_ponere_actio(c, name) and
      register a handler for the same name with actio_registrare(reg,
      name, fn, ctx). The dispensator walks the ascent from the target
      upward; at each componens that names an action with a handler
      registered in its spatium it calls the ActioFn, and VERUM means
      consumed: the ascent stops. FALSUM lets the ancestors try. The
      nodus argument is the componens whose action matched, which may
      be an ancestor of the event's target.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I register the same action name twice or replace a handler?
    <responsum symbolum="actio_registrare actio_registrum_vacare">No.
      actio_registrare returns FALSUM for a name already registered in
      the host spatium, for an empty name (interning returns NIHIL for
      an empty string) and for a NIHIL fn. There is no single
      unregister: actio_registrum_vacare empties the whole registry
      (keeping its memory) so it can be rebuilt.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>who owns the action name strings, and must a lookup use an interned chorda?
    <responsum symbolum="actio_registrare chorda_internare_ex_literis actio_invenire actio_registrum_miscere">actio_registrare
      interns the C string into the registry's internamentum, so a
      string literal or a temporary buffer is fine. Lookups compare by
      content (chorda_aequalis), so actio_invenire accepts any chorda,
      interned or not. Merging copies the source's name chordas by
      reference, so the source's internamentum must outlive the target
      registry (the header asks for the same internamentum).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can a widget inside a mounted pane trigger a handler registered in the host?
    <responsum symbolum="actio_invenire_in_spatio actio_registrum_miscere_in_spatio componens_spatium">Not
      one registered in a different spatium. The dispensator looks the
      name up with actio_invenire_in_spatio in the componens' effective
      spatium (componens_spatium: the nearest ancestor that sets one),
      and the lookup is strict. If nothing matches there it skips that
      componens and keeps ascending, so an ancestor in another spatium
      can still handle its own action. Put a pane's handlers into the
      host registry with actio_registrum_miscere_in_spatio; entries
      that already carry a spatium keep it. The spatium chorda is
      stored as given and must outlive the registry.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I find action names in my tree that have no handler, or handlers nobody uses?
    <responsum symbolum="actio_non_registratae actio_non_relatae">actio_non_registratae
      returns the names the tree references with no handler in the
      componens' spatium (an error for lint L10), and actio_non_relatae
      the registered names no componens references (a warning). Both
      return a Xar of chorda, each name once per spatium, allocated
      from the piscina you pass. The chordas carry the name only, not
      its spatium. A NIHIL registry or piscina gives NIHIL; a NIHIL
      tree gives an empty Xar (and every handler counts as
      unreferenced).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why are insula writes made inside an action handler attributed to the action's name?
    <responsum symbolum="insula_scriptorem_ponere ActioFn" via="lib/dispensator.c">The
      dispensator sets the insula writer to the componens' action name
      with insula_scriptorem_ponere just before calling the handler and
      clears it right after, so every write the handler makes is
      owned by that action. Handlers do no I/O (lint L5): they change
      state through the InsulaRepositorium and the motus they are
      handed, and effects travel as data.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
